  "query_preview": "/Through Wednesday/...\nIssued at 1251 PM MDT Wed Sep 9 2026\n\nA quiet day is expected across the forecast area today behind this \nearly morning's cold front. Dare we say that fall is trying to make \nitself known, despite a few more 90 degree days lurking in our \nforecast? \n\nFor today, temperatures will be at or slightly below \nnormal across the plains with the cooler air mass in place. The \ncooler air won't pack enough of a punch to make it to our mountain \nvalleys, where afternoon high temps will still remain about five to \nten degrees above normal. As evident on water vapor imagery, much \ndrier air will be in place across the forecast area, as westerly \nflow aloft will keep the monsoonal moisture plume flowing into \nsoutheastern Colorado, on the periphery of our southern most \ncounties. CAMs show storms developing along a cold front in \nsoutheastern Colorado tonight. A few may manage to move north into \nsouthern Lincoln County late tonight. With PW values around 1-1.5\", \nany storms that do move into the area will be capable of some heavy \nrainfall. \n\nWarm and dry conditions are expected on Thursday as flow aloft turns \nmore northwesterly, and 700 mb temps warm a few degrees. This will \ntranslate to a few degrees of warming at the surface, bringing \nafternoon high temps back to above normal across the forecast area. \nRelative humidities will drop into the single digits in our high \nmountain valleys and there will be some patchy elevated to near \ncritical fire weather conditions, mainly for North Park and some \nlocalized areas along the Continental Divide. \n\nMid-level moisture will start to work its way back into the area on \nFriday as a shortwave moves across the northern Rockies and flow \naloft becomes more westerly, once again. There may be enough \nmoisture in place for some high-based showers in the \nafternoon/evening, but instability looks lacking for anything \nsubstantial at this time. Folks across the plains have a good shot \nat ending their work week with temperatures at or around the 90 \ndegree mark before another cold front slides south across Colorado \nFriday night that will bring Saturday's high temps back closer to \nnormal. \n\nThe northern tier will remain active through the weekend, with a \nmore pronounced shortwave expected to move into the Pacific \nNorthwest on Saturday and traverse the northern Rockies on Sunday. \nThis will turn flow aloft slightly more southwesterly through the \nday on Saturday and allow us to tap back into some mid-level \nPacific moisture. Afternoon showers and storm activity will \nincrease, with isolated to scattered showers/storms possible each \nafternoon. One more summerlike warmup is expected on Sunday, with\nafternoon highs warming into the low to mid 90s along the urban\ncorridor. Another cold front will push south Sunday night cooling\nthings back down to near normal temperatures for Monday. The \nforecast high for Denver International Airport is 93 degrees on \nSunday, which would put us within a few degrees of the record high\nfor this day (96 is the current September 13th record, last set \nin 2018). Looking at ensembles, this may end up being one of, if \nnot the last 90 degree day of the season, as there is a definite \ntrend of cooler temperatures (sub 90 degrees) present in the \nlatest ensemble outputs (though there is certainly plenty of time \nfor this to change!).",
